One of the world's greatest Bronze Age gold collections has been swiped, almost in its entirety, from Villena Museum. A highly professional group of thieves were in and out in four minutes flat before the museum opened.

Thieves on Thursday raided one of the world's greatest Bronze Age gold collections in the latest of a series of high-profile heists on European museums in recent months.&#160;

The early morning raid on Villena Museum in eastern Spain lasted just a few minutes.&#160;

A group of thieves described by one official&#160;as "super-fast and super-professional" made off with almost the entire Treasure of Villena&#160;collection, leaving behind only a few items.&#160;

"The alarm at the Villena Museum went off around 5:20 a.m.," said a spokesman for the town council, adding that within four minutes, they had all left the building again in an extremely rapid and professional operation.&#160;

The collection of Bronze Age gold, some of which we now know hailed from a meteorite, was a centerpiece at the&#160;small museum in the Alicante province.&#160;

"They've taken most of the treasure," the mayor of Villena, Fulgencio Cerdan told journalists. "We are absolutely devastated, I'm even trembling. The truth is that for those of us from Villena, this destroys us. It's part of our heritage, our wealth."&#160;

The thieves arrived in several vehicles and apparently entered the museum "through a window," according to the Spanish government's deputy representative in Alicante, Manuel Pineda.&#160;

Mayor Cerdan said that only three silver vessels, a bracelet, an earring and one of four crowns in the collection — which the thieves appeared to have "dropped" while making their exit — remained behind.

&#160;"All security systems were activated," according to the Villena spokesman, who insisted there had been "no failure" by the museum. &#160;

The Treasure of Villena is a collection of more than 50 mainly gold objects, discovered in 1963 by archaeologist Jose Maria Soler in a sunken vessel&#160;in a dried out riverbed.&#160;

The collection has been exhibited beyond Villena in cities including Madrid, Alicante, Tokyo and Kyoto, and two sets of replicas were later&#160;made to showcase the collection without taking it away from the armored display&#160;at the Villena&#160;Archaeological Museum.&#160;

The relics' historical value is incalculable, although thieves might struggle to sell them even on the black market after having famously lifted them from a well-known display. The mayor said the gold alone would be worth around&#160;€1.7 million or $2 million.&#160;

